msgid "Cyrillic names in Latin letters"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1710
msgid "Bulgaria has an official transliteration (the Transliteration Act of 2009): `ж` -> `zh`, `ц` -> `ts`, `ч` -> `ch`, `ш` -> `sh`, `щ` -> `sht`, `ъ` -> `a`, `ю` -> `yu`, `я` -> `ya`, ... A dictionary of single letters to strings, used with [`translate`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.translate):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1727
msgid "[`str.maketrans`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.maketrans) accepts a dictionary of **one character -> any string**, so one letter can become two or three. (The full rules have a few exceptions, for example `-ия` at the end of a word is `-ia`: `София` -> `Sofia`, not `Sofiya`.)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1731
msgid "**One letter, two forms.** Another trap of text from many sources: the same letter can be stored in two ways - `\"й\"` as one character, or `\"и\"` plus a combining mark. They look the same, but are not `==`. [`unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", s)`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/unicodedata.html#unicodedata.normalize) brings both to the same form:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1746
msgid "Add the rule \"`ия` at the end of a word -> `ia`\" to the transliteration: first replace it, then [`translate`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.translate). Check it with `София` and `Мария`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1761
msgid "(The ASCII letters `ia` pass through [`translate`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.translate) unchanged.)"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1764
msgid "Greedy and lazy quantifiers"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1766
msgid "`*` also matches **nothing** - zero digits are \"0 or more\". With [`findall`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.findall), that gives empty strings between the matches:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1776
msgid "When you want \"at least one\", write `+`, not `*`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1778
msgid "**Greedy.** Quantifiers are **greedy**: they take as much text as they can, and give back only what is needed for the rest of the pattern to match:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1788
msgid "**Lazy.** A `?` after the quantifier makes it **lazy**: as little as possible."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1798
msgid "`.*` takes too much"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1799
msgid "`.*` is the most common cause of a wrong match: it runs to the end of the line, then steps back. When the text between two markers cannot contain the marker, write a class with `^`: `\"[^\"]*\"`, `\\([^)]*\\)`."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1806
msgid "In `\"(valuation report from 12.06.2026) (checked 2026-06-15)\"`, find the text inside each pair of brackets. First try `\\(.*\\)`, then make it lazy."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1820
msgid "More functions of `re`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1822
msgid "**[`finditer`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.finditer): the match objects.** [`finditer`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.finditer) gives the **match objects**, with their positions - when you need more than the text:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1833
msgid "**[`split`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.split) with a pattern.** [`re.split`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.split) cuts at every match - when the separators vary:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1842
msgid "**`re.MULTILINE`.** With a text of many lines, `^` and `$` mean the start and the end of the **whole** text. The flag `re.MULTILINE` (`re.M`) makes them work **per line**:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1857
msgid "**[`re.compile`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.compile): a pattern used many times.** [`re.compile`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.compile) turns a pattern into an object with the same methods. Give it a name at the top of the script, and use it in a loop - it reads well and is checked once:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1874
msgid "**[`re.subn`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.subn): count the replacements.** [`re.subn`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.subn) works like [`re.sub`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.sub), and also returns the number of replacements - useful in a cleaning report:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1884
msgid "**[`re.escape`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.escape).** When a text that you look for has special characters, [`re.escape(text)`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.escape) puts the backslashes for you:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1895
msgid "Split `\"retail;sme , corporate,project\"` into the four words with one [`re.split`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.split), without spaces around the words."
